course: geography
lesson: 44
title: Earthquakes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Rocks along a crack in the crust, a fault, want to slide past each other but are stuck; the strain builds up for years until suddenly they slip, and the jolt shakes the ground: that is an earthquake.
explain: Bend a ruler between your hands: it bends and bends, then snaps, and your hands jump; the rocks store energy the same way and release it in seconds.
explain: The shaking spreads out as waves: fast P waves arrive first, slower S waves next, and rolling surface waves last, which do the most damage.
explain: Shaking ground rarely kills anyone by itself; falling buildings do, so the best protection is buildings designed to bend and sway instead of crack.
explain: Nobody can yet say on which day an earthquake will strike, but scientists can map where the risk is high and how strong the quakes there can be.
fact: An earthquake is the sudden shaking when rocks along a fault slip after strain has built up.
fact: The point underground where the rock slips is the focus, and the point on the surface right above it is the epicentre, where the shaking is usually strongest.
fact: Earthquakes send out fast P waves that push and pull, slower S waves that shake side to side, and surface waves that do most damage; a machine called a seismograph records them.
fact: Earthquake size is given by magnitude, and each step up means about 32 times more energy released.
fact: The strongest earthquake ever recorded was in Chile in 1960, magnitude about 9.5.
fact: In 1755 an earthquake, a tsunami and fires destroyed much of Lisbon, killing tens of thousands of people.
fact: Most earthquake deaths are caused by collapsing buildings, not by the shaking ground itself.
fact: Earthquake-proof buildings use steel frames, flexible joints and even rubber or sliding bases to soak up shaking.
fact: Scientists cannot predict the exact day of an earthquake, but they can map which places are at risk.
fact: Britain has hundreds of small earthquakes a year, but almost all are too weak to feel.
fact: After a big earthquake come many smaller aftershocks, sometimes for months or years.
fact: In an earthquake, people are taught to drop to the floor, take cover under a sturdy table and hold on until the shaking stops.
exercise: What causes an earthquake? | Rocks along a fault suddenly slipping after strain builds up | Thunder from big storms shaking the ground from above it | The Moon pulling the land up and down twice every day | Underground rivers bursting through the rock under towns
exercise: What is the focus of an earthquake? | The point underground where the rock slips | The point on the surface right above the slip | The town that gets the most damage from the shaking | The machine that measures how hard the ground shakes
exercise: What is the epicentre? | The point on the surface right above the focus | The deepest point the earthquake reaches underground | The centre of the Earth, where all quakes start | The edge of the area that shook
exercise: Which earthquake waves arrive first? | P waves that push and pull | S waves that shake side to side | Surface waves that roll the ground | All the waves arrive at once
exercise: Which earthquake waves usually do the most damage? | Surface waves | P waves, the first to arrive | Radio waves | Sound waves from the crack
exercise: How do S waves move the ground? | They shake it side to side | They push and pull it like a spring | They lift it straight up only | They do not move the ground at all
exercise: How much more energy does a magnitude 7 release than a magnitude 6? | About 32 times more | About twice as much, just a bit more | Exactly one more unit of energy | About 1,000 times more
exercise: Where and when was the strongest earthquake ever recorded? | Chile, 1960, magnitude about 9.5 | Japan, 2011, magnitude about 12 | California, 1906, magnitude about 10 | Lisbon, 1755, magnitude about 11
exercise: What destroyed much of Lisbon in 1755? | An earthquake, a tsunami and fires | A volcano and a lava flow down the hill | A hurricane and floods | A long war and a siege by the Spanish
exercise: What causes most earthquake deaths? | Collapsing buildings | The ground opening and swallowing people | Lava coming out of the cracks | The loud noise of the quake
exercise: How do earthquake-proof buildings stay up? | Steel frames, flexible joints and sliding bases soak up shaking | Very thick stone walls that are too heavy to ever move at all | Building them as tall as possible so they sway above the shaking | Painting them with a special paint
exercise: Can scientists predict the exact day of an earthquake? | No, but they can map which places are at risk | Yes, always a week before | Yes, by watching the Moon | No, and they cannot say where they happen either
exercise: Does Britain have earthquakes? | Yes, hundreds of small ones a year, mostly too weak to feel | No, never, Britain is too far from any plate edge for that | Yes, a big deadly one every year somewhere in the country | Only in Scotland and only in winter
exercise: What are aftershocks? | Smaller earthquakes after a big one, for months or years | The deep rumbling sound you hear after an earthquake | The fast waves that arrive before the main quake hits | Tsunamis that follow every earthquake
exercise: What should you do indoors when the ground starts shaking? | Drop, take cover under a sturdy table and hold on | Run outside down the stairs at once, as fast as you can | Stand by a big window so you can see what is happening | Get in the lift and go down
test: Jake says: "In an earthquake the ground opens up and swallows whole cities." Put him right. | That's rare; most deaths come from buildings falling down | He's right, that's what kills most people in a big quake | The ground opens and lava pours out of the cracks instead | Cities sink slowly into the sea instead
test: "my mate says a 6 is just a bit worse than a 5, like one more" Is he right? | No, a 6 releases about 32 times more energy | Yes, it's exactly one unit more energy, a bit worse | No, a 6 is a thousand times weaker than a 5 | Yes, the numbers are just names
test: A seismograph 500 km away records two jolts a minute apart. Why two? | The fast P waves arrive before the slower S waves | Two earthquakes happened at exactly that same moment | The machine is broken and counts every quake twice | The second jolt is an echo from the Moon
test: In a disaster film a scientist says: "The earthquake will hit at 3:15 on Tuesday." What's wrong? | Nobody can predict the exact time of an earthquake | Earthquakes only happen at night, never in the afternoon | Scientists can predict them to the second | Nothing, that is exactly how real warnings work
test: What would happen if a city built all its houses from heavy bricks with no steel? | A strong quake would bring many down and cause many deaths | They would be the safest houses possible, as bricks are heavy | The bricks would soak up all the shaking like a sponge does | Nothing, bricks cannot fall down
test: "crap, the house shook a bit last night in Lincolnshire, is that even possible in England?" | Yes, Britain has small earthquakes and a few are felt | No, it must have been a lorry, England has never had one | Yes, but only if there is a volcano somewhere near you | No, earthquakes only happen in hot countries
test: A week after a big quake the ground shakes again. Mia says a new disaster is starting. What's most likely? | It is an aftershock, common for months after | It is a volcano about to erupt | It is a sign the first quake was fake | It is a tsunami arriving late
test: Where would the shaking usually be strongest? | Near the epicentre, right above the focus | Exactly halfway round the world from the focus | In the city furthest from the fault | On the other side of the Earth, opposite the focus
test: Why did Lisbon in 1755 suffer so much more than the shaking alone? | A tsunami and fires followed the earthquake | A volcano erupted right under the city centre | The quake lasted for a whole year without stopping | The city had no buildings at all
test: Your little sister bends a plastic ruler until it snaps and her hands jump. How is this like an earthquake? | Strain builds up slowly, then is released all at once | The ruler shows how lava squeezes out of a volcano | It shows how the Moon pulls the plates up and down | It isn't like an earthquake at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the epicentre of an earthquake? | The point upon the surface situated directly above the focus | The point within the crust at which the rupture originates | The location at which the greatest number of casualties occurs | The point upon the opposite side of the planet from the focus
exam: Identify the principal cause of fatalities in the event of a major earthquake. | The collapse of buildings and other structures | The opening of fissures which engulf the population | The emission of lava from ruptured faults | The noise generated by the movement of the ground
exam: It is given that an earthquake of magnitude 7 has occurred. Ascertain the approximate energy released relative to an earthquake of magnitude 6. | Approximately thirty-two times greater | Approximately twice as great | Exactly equal in quantity to the former | Approximately one-tenth as great in quantity
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the forecasting of earthquakes. | The precise date cannot be predicted, although regions of hazard may be mapped | The precise date and hour may be predicted with confidence one week in advance | Neither the date nor the region of hazard can be determined | Earthquakes occur only upon those dates determined by the phases of the Moon
